<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeong Mong-ju</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1337">1337</a>–<a href="/years/1392">1392</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeong Mong-ju was a Korean Neo-Confucian scholar, diplomat and statesman of the late Goryeo period. He led missions to Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and promoted Confucian ritual and education. Remaining loyal to Goryeo, he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a>'s plans to found a new dynasty and was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> in 1392 by men of Yi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejong-of-joseon">Yi Bang-won</a>, becoming a lasting symbol of loyalty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeong-mong-ju.jpg" alt="Jeong Mong-ju" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yi Han-cheol · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeong_Mongju.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
